<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Ryswick</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1697">1697</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Peace of Ryswick · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war">Nine Years' War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Ryswick, signed in September and October 1697 at Rijswijk near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a>, ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war">Nine Years' War</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the Grand Alliance. France returned most of its gains since 1679 but kept Strasbourg, and recognised William III as king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> accepted French possession of the western third of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>, which became the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ryswick.jpg" alt="Treaty of Ryswick" width="320" height="272" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: engraver is Jan van Vianen, whose name appears in the lower left corner · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rijswijk_Netherlands_-_Huis_ter_Nieuwburg_by_P._Schenck.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Nine Years' War was financially crippling for its participants, partly because armies had grown from an average of 25,000 in 1648 to over 100,000 by 1697. The war absorbed 80% of English state revenue in the period. The 1690s were also the coldest point of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/little-ice-age">Little Ice Age</a>, and harvests failed across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in 1695 and 1696; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-europe">Northern Europe</a> an estimated 5 to 15% of the population starved. Fighting largely ended in Europe after 1695, but King William's War continued in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadia">Acadia</a> and the French captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york-factory">York Factory</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Talks were dominated by the Spanish succession. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-spain">Charles II of Spain</a> was unlikely to leave an heir, and the leading claimants were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> and Emperor Leopold I. Louis, unable to impose his solution, tried to prevent discussion of it by dividing the Grand Alliance. He made a separate peace with Savoy in the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> of 1696, returned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and agreed to recognise William III rather than the exiled James II.</p><p>Formal talks were held at the Huis ter Nieuwburg in Ryswick, mediated by the Swedish diplomat Baron Lilliënrot. Leopold would agree only to a ceasefire, since the terms left the inheritance open. William and Louis sent personal representatives, the Earl of Portland and Marshal Louis-François de Boufflers, who finalised terms near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> in June 1697. On 20 September France signed peace treaties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and a ceasefire with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, and on 21 September a treaty of peace and commerce with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>. Leopold delayed, then signed on 30 October after William threatened to dissolve the Alliance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> gave back Freiburg, Breisach, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippsburg">Philippsburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> to the Empire and evacuated Catalonia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kortrijk">Kortrijk</a>, but kept Strasbourg. Louis withdrew support from the Jacobites and gave up claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> and the Palatinate. In North America France regained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadia">Acadia</a>, though low-level conflict persisted, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> returned Pondichéry to France.</p><p>All sides took the peace as a truce, and the death of Charles II in 1700 led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>. The Partition Treaties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> in 1698 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1700 failed to prevent it. The war showed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> could not impose its aims without allies. Historians treat Ryswick as a turning point in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>'s rise as a maritime power, with London replacing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s commercial centre. After James II died in 1701, Louis proclaimed his son rightful king of England.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Peace of Ryswick” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the <b>Peace of Ryswick</b> in 1697 Spain tacitly recognised the French presence in the western third of the island.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The choir of Christopher Wren's new St Paul's opened for worship with a thanksgiving service for the <b>Peace of Ryswick</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-2">2 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It kept its Lutheran churches and local institutions, but became part of the Kingdom of France, confirmed by the <b>Treaty of Ryswick</b> in 1697.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-strasbourg">Free Imperial City of Strasbourg</a></li></ul></section>
